window.BOOK = {"cover": {"series": "AN ONCHAIN EDUCATION SERIES", "title": "WALLY'S RWA TEXTBOOK", "sponsor": "SPONSORED BY RWA FOUNDATION", "handle": "@wallycollection", "blurb": ["Most RWA takes are a chart and a promise. This isn't that.", "Wally covers the vocabulary, the asset classes, and how to tell when a chart's doing more work than the data behind it.", "No price calls. No hopium.", "Just what you need before you put money into anything with 'onchain' in the name."]}, "chapters": [{"n": 1, "id": "foundations", "name": "Foundations", "lessons": [{"id": "1.1", "page": 1, "title": "What Is an RWA?", "sub": "Real-World Asset", "body": ["An RWA is any asset that exists offchain first, a treasury bill, a private loan, a building, a bar of gold, and gets a token on a blockchain that represents a claim on it. The token moves at internet speed. The asset backing it does not change."], "point": "Tokenization does not change what the asset is worth. It changes who can hold it, how fast it settles, and what you can build on top of it.", "chapter": 1, "note": "a building. in a wallet. (sort of)", "t": 32.86}, {"id": "1.2", "page": 2, "title": "Tokenization", "sub": "Asset to token", "body": ["Tokenization takes an asset, say a US Treasury bill or a slice of private credit, and gives it a token on a blockchain. The asset does not change. What changes is how ownership moves. A token can settle in minutes, trade at any hour, and split into pieces far smaller than the original instrument ever allowed."], "point": "The RWA is still the asset. Tokenization is just the wrapper that moves it differently.", "chapter": 1, "note": "same burrito. new tortilla.", "t": 41.43}, {"id": "1.3", "page": 3, "title": "Onchain", "sub": "Where the claim actually lives", "body": ["Onchain just means the record sits on a blockchain instead of an issuer's internal ledger. The building doesn't move. The loan doesn't move. What changes hands is the claim, who owns it, how much, and what happened to it last. That settlement happens in minutes instead of days, at any hour, and anyone can check it without calling the issuer to confirm it."], "point": "Onchain doesn't make the asset better. It makes the ownership record harder to fudge.", "chapter": 1, "note": "nice try, eraser", "t": 50.0}, {"id": "1.4", "page": 4, "title": "Custody", "sub": "Who's actually holding the asset", "body": ["Custody answers the question everyone eventually asks. Who's actually holding this? Tokenization creates the claim. Onchain makes it easy to track. Custody is the arrangement behind both, the party holding the real treasury bill, the real gold bar, the real loan file, and what happens to it if the platform vanishes tomorrow."], "point": "A token is only as good as the custody behind it. Weak custody turns a claim into a lawsuit.", "chapter": 1, "note": "*gavel noises*", "t": 58.57}, {"id": "1.5", "page": 5, "title": "Transfer Agent", "sub": "Who's allowed to hold the token", "body": ["Transfer agent is the record keeper nobody thinks about until something breaks. Custody proves someone holds the treasury bill. Transfer agent decides who is allowed to hold the token that represents it. Every transfer gets checked against KYC, accreditation, and jurisdiction rules before it clears. Onchain settlement is fast. Whether that settlement is legally valid is the transfer agent's call.", "Not every RWA appoints a separate transfer agent. Some build the same rules into the token itself: an allowlist of verified wallets, and a transfer function that checks KYC, accreditation and jurisdiction before anything moves. The role does not disappear. It becomes code, and the issuer still answers for it."], "point": "A token can move in seconds. Whether that move counts under the law is decided by the rules behind it: a transfer agent, or compliance built into the token. Never the blockchain alone.", "chapter": 1, "note": "bouncer energy", "t": 67.14}, {"id": "1.6", "page": 6, "title": "Total Value (vs TVL)", "sub": "Two numbers that get used like they're interchangeable", "body": ["Total Value counts every dollar of a real-world asset that's been tokenized: sitting in a wallet, held in custody, wherever it lives onchain. TVL counts something narrower, the value actually deposited into a protocol's smart contracts and doing something. A tokenized treasury sitting untouched in someone's wallet is real. It isn't locked anywhere. Reports that blend the two make dormant supply look like active capital."], "point": "Total Value measures what exists onchain. TVL measures what's actually working. Conflating the two turns idle supply into a story about demand.", "chapter": 1, "note": "that's not demand. that's a nap.", "t": 75.71}, {"id": "1.7", "page": 7, "title": "Issuance vs Distribution", "sub": "Creating a token and getting it into hands are two different jobs", "body": ["Issuance is minting. The issuer creates the token and the supply exists the moment it's created. Distribution is separate, who actually ended up holding it. A raise can be fully issued on day one while sitting almost entirely with the issuer and a handful of early backers. That's real issuance. It isn't real distribution. Issuance size alone is not proof of demand."], "point": "Issuance tells you an asset exists. Distribution tells you who actually wanted it. One is a mint. The other is a market.", "chapter": 1, "note": "issued: 1B. holders: the founder's group chat", "t": 84.29}, {"id": "1.8", "page": 8, "title": "Smart Contract Wrapper", "sub": "The code layer that decides how a claim actually moves", "body": ["Smart contract wrapper is the code that turns a legal claim into a token a wallet can hold. The contract doesn't create the asset, a lawyer and a custodian already did that. It encodes the rules, who can hold the token, whether it can be frozen, how transfers get checked. Two tokens can represent the same asset and behave completely differently onchain depending on the wrapper."], "point": "The wrapper doesn't make the claim real. It decides how that claim moves, who can hold it, and who has the power to stop it.", "chapter": 1, "note": "same gold. different off-switch.", "t": 92.86}], "tagline": "the words people misuse on purpose"}, {"n": 2, "id": "equities", "name": "Tokenized Equities", "lessons": [{"id": "2.1", "page": 9, "title": "Tokenized Equity", "sub": "A real share, wrapped so it can move onchain", "body": ["A tokenized equity is a real share in a real company, wrapped in a token that can move onchain. The stock still exists in the traditional system, held by a custodian or transfer agent under securities law. The token is a claim on that share, not a synthetic bet on its price. That distinction matters more than it sounds. A tokenized equity gives you exposure with faster settlement and global reach. A synthetic product gives you a promise from whoever issued it, backed by nothing but their word. Wrapping doesn't change what you own. It changes how far it can travel and how fast."], "point": "A token is a wrapper on legal ownership, not a new asset class of its own. Read the wrapper, not just the ticker.", "chapter": 2, "note": "backed by nothing but their word", "t": 111.43}, {"id": "2.2", "page": 10, "title": "Three Types of Tokenized Stock", "sub": "Native, custodial, and synthetic don't mean the same thing.", "body": ["Tokenized equity comes in three types. Native tokens make the holder the actual shareholder of record, the issuer authorizes the tokenization and records it directly in the company's own share register. Custodial tokens work differently. A regulated custodian holds the real share, and the token represents a claim against it, real exposure to the asset, but the custodian stays the legal owner, not you. Synthetic tokens hold nothing behind them at all. They track the share's price through oracles and smart contracts, with no share sitting anywhere. Check which type you're holding before you treat it like a stock."], "point": "A tokenized stock is not one claim. It's three, and only one makes you a shareholder.", "chapter": 2, "note": "synthetic = vibes with a price feed", "t": 120.0}, {"id": "2.3", "page": 11, "title": "The Wrapper Decides Who Can Buy", "sub": "Same share, different rulebook, different buyer.", "body": ["Two tokens can represent the same company and still be sold under completely different rules. In the US, Reg D covers private placements, accredited investors only, with a holding period before the token can move on. Reg A+ allows retail participation but caps the raise and requires SEC qualification first, which takes months. Reg S sits outside the US entirely, non-US buyers only, with a compliance period before those tokens can touch American hands. Most tokenized equity offerings run Reg D and Reg S side by side, splitting the buyer pool by passport. The wrapper is not paperwork. It sets who can hold the token, how long they wait, and where it can trade."], "point": "Before asking what a token represents, ask which rulebook it was issued under. That answers who can actually own it.", "chapter": 2, "note": "splitting the buyer pool by passport", "t": 129.29}, {"id": "2.4", "page": 12, "title": "Voting Rights and Corporate Actions", "sub": "A vote and a corporate action are two different problems to solve.", "body": ["A tokenized share can carry a vote, and separately it can carry a path through corporate actions, and issuers do not always solve both. Voting means the holder can weigh in on matters put to shareholders. Corporate actions are everything that happens to the share afterward: a stock split, a tender offer, a merger, a buyback. Each one requires the issuer to notify holders, collect elections, and update the token, none of which happens automatically onchain. Some issuers build this pipeline properly. Others pass through a vote but go silent when a real corporate action hits, leaving token holders outside the process the paperwork promised them."], "point": "A pass-through vote is easy to promise. Ask how the issuer handles the next merger.", "chapter": 2, "note": "merger day. token holders: not invited.", "t": 137.86}], "tagline": "a stock in a wrapper is still a stock (usually)"}, {"n": 3, "id": "market", "name": "Market Structure", "lessons": [{"id": "3.1", "page": 13, "title": "Secondary Liquidity", "sub": "Owning it is one thing. Selling it is another.", "body": ["Secondary liquidity is whether you can sell what you hold, at a fair price, in the size you want, on the day you choose. Issuance is easy now. The exit is not. Buyer pools stay small because the wrapper limits who qualifies, transfer rules block wallets that are not whitelisted, and venues sit scattered across chains that never share order flow. What shows up instead is spread, price moving on small size, and a queue standing in for a market. Watch traded volume against total value. Value tells you what was issued. Volume tells you what can leave."], "point": "Liquidity is not a property of the token. It is a function of how many qualified buyers can reach it, and how fast.", "chapter": 3, "note": "SELL ▸ … *cricket noises*", "t": 150.0}, {"id": "3.2", "page": 14, "title": "Settlement (T+0 vs T+2)", "sub": "Speed is not the same thing as finality.", "body": ["Settlement is the moment a trade actually finishes, ownership and payment changing hands at the same time, with no path back to undo it. Traditional equities settle T+1 now, cash and shares moving through custodians and clearinghouses a day after the trade is agreed. Tokenized assets can settle T+0, the same instant the trade executes, because the token and the payment move in a single transaction instead of two systems reconciling later. But T+0 only holds if both legs are onchain. If the token moves instantly and the cash still runs through a bank wire, you have not removed settlement risk. You have just moved it to the leg nobody is watching."], "point": "T+0 is a claim about the token leg. Ask what settles the cash leg, and how fast, before you call it instant.", "chapter": 3, "note": "⚡ token: done. 🐌 cash: 'processing'", "t": 158.57}, {"id": "3.3", "page": 15, "title": "Order Books vs AMMs", "sub": "Two ways to find a price, built for different assets.", "body": ["Order books match buyers and sellers directly, each side posting a price and size while a matching engine looks for where the two sides cross. That structure works when enough participants are trading at once, keeping the book full and the spread tight. AMMs replace the matching engine with a formula and a pool of tokens, letting anyone trade against the pool at whatever price the formula sets, no counterparty required. That works when trading is thin, but only if someone is willing to fund the pool. RWAs mostly trade a handful of times a day, not several times a second. A pool holding an illiquid asset just leaves liquidity providers exposed to stale prices."], "point": "Order books and AMMs both assume trading that never stops. RWA volume stops constantly, and that mismatch is the bottleneck nobody prices in.", "chapter": 3, "note": "LPs holding stale prices like milk in the sun", "t": 167.14}, {"id": "3.4", "page": 16, "title": "Price Discovery Challenges", "sub": "A price only matters if someone actually traded at it.", "body": ["Price discovery works when enough trades happen close together that the market can triangulate where value sits right now. Public equities do this every few seconds because volume never really stops. Most RWAs don't get that luxury. A tokenized treasury or private credit note might trade once a day, once a week, or not until the next rebalance. The last trade gets treated as current even when it is hours or days old. Oracles and NAV feeds fill the gap with estimates, but an estimate is not the same thing as a market actually finding a price."], "point": "A price only means something if someone actually traded at it recently. For most RWAs, that condition rarely holds.", "chapter": 3, "note": "price: 'current'. last trade: last tuesday.", "t": 175.71}], "tagline": "buying is easy. leaving is the plot twist."}, {"n": 4, "id": "treasuries", "name": "Treasuries", "lessons": [{"id": "4.1", "page": 17, "title": "What Tokenized Treasuries Are", "sub": "The same T-bill, wrapped so it moves at the speed of a wallet.", "body": ["Tokenized treasuries are shares in a fund that holds short duration U.S. Treasury bills, represented as a token instead of a line in a brokerage statement. The underlying asset does not change. A T-bill is still a T-bill, settled through the same custody chain it always was.", "What changes is the wrapper. Ownership lives onchain and moves the moment a wallet to wallet transfer clears, instead of waiting on a fund administrator to update a spreadsheet overnight. That simple credit risk made treasuries the sector's proof of concept."], "point": "Tokenized treasuries did not reinvent the T-bill. They rebuilt the pipe it travels through.", "chapter": 4, "note": "the T-bill is unbothered", "t": 187.86}, {"id": "4.2", "page": 18, "title": "Who's Building It", "sub": "Managers, issuers, and the rails underneath them.", "body": ["Three groups build in this space. Traditional asset managers tokenize funds they already run, BlackRock's BUIDL and Franklin Templeton's BENJI sit directly on top of treasury portfolios these firms have managed for decades. Independent issuers like Ondo and Superstate built the other way, structuring the fund onchain first and sourcing custody and compliance from partners around it. Underneath both, infrastructure providers handle the transfer agent function, tracking who owns what and enforcing who is allowed to hold it. The names compete for flow. The three roles do not change."], "point": "Know whether you're holding a fund's own token or someone else's wrapper around it.", "kind": "builders", "chapter": 4, "note": "the names compete. the roles don't change.", "t": 196.43}, {"id": "4.3", "page": 19, "title": "Yield Mechanics", "sub": "How treasury income moves from the fund to your wallet.", "body": ["Yield on a tokenized treasury fund starts with the fund's actual T-bill holdings. Interest accrues daily as those bills mature and roll into new ones, and the fund's net asset value moves up in step. Issuers pass that accrual to holders one of two ways, rebasing tokens like BUIDL mint new tokens into your wallet each day so the price stays fixed, while accruing tokens like BENJI and USDY let the price itself climb. Either way there is a short lag between when the underlying bills earn interest and when it reaches the token, usually a day, sometimes tied to a broker settlement cycle. The yield is real. It is just not instant."], "point": "The token just reports the yield. The T-bills underneath are doing the actual work.", "chapter": 4, "note": "the token takes credit for the group project", "t": 205.0}, {"id": "4.4", "page": 20, "title": "Redemption & Custody Chain", "sub": "Getting your cash back means unwinding the same chain in reverse.", "body": ["Redemption begins with a request submitted through the issuer's platform. The fund then sells T-bills or draws down cash reserves to cover the payout, while the transfer agent updates the official ownership ledger. The custodian holding the underlying bills releases funds to the fund's bank account, and only then does the token get burned and cash or stablecoin move to the redeemer. The process usually takes one business day, sometimes longer near quarter end when treasury desks are busiest. Each link in that chain needs an institution standing behind it."], "point": "Burning the token is the last step. Everything that matters happens before it.", "chapter": 4, "note": "the 🔥 is the receipt, not the process", "t": 213.57}], "tagline": "the boring asset that proved the point"}, {"n": 5, "id": "yield", "name": "Yield & Returns", "lessons": [{"id": "5.1", "page": 21, "title": "Advertised vs. Realized Yield", "sub": "The number on the landing page is rarely the number you actually earn.", "body": ["Advertised yield is the headline APY shown on a protocol's front page. Realized yield is what actually lands in a holder's wallet after fees, slippage on entry and exit, and the gap between when yield accrues and when it is actually distributed.", "The spread widens when a fund holds cash buffers that earn less than the underlying assets, when management fees sit on top of gross yield, or when the headline number was measured on a single strong day instead of averaged over time."], "point": "The advertised number gets the clicks. The realized number gets you paid.", "chapter": 5, "note": "18%* *measured on one great tuesday", "t": 232.86}, {"id": "5.2", "page": 22, "title": "Lockups & Redemption Windows", "sub": "Every quoted yield comes attached to a schedule for getting your money back.", "body": ["Lockups and redemption windows are two different constraints that often get discussed as one. A lockup is a fixed period when you cannot exit at all. A redemption window is the recurring schedule that decides when an exit request is actually processed. Tokenized treasury funds tend to redeem daily or on the next business day. Private credit and real estate vehicles often run monthly or quarterly windows with a notice period attached, so a request submitted today may not settle for weeks. A fund that promises faster redemption than its collateral can deliver holds idle cash to cover the gap, and that cash lowers the yield you were quoted."], "point": "Read the redemption terms first. The yield only counts if you can reach it.", "chapter": 5, "note": "exit opens: next quarter. notice period: also yes.", "t": 241.43}, {"id": "5.3", "page": 23, "title": "Waterfall & Tranching Basics", "sub": "Two tokens can sit on the same collateral and carry completely different risk.", "body": ["A waterfall is the order in which cash from a pool of assets gets paid out. Interest and principal reach the senior tranche first, then the mezzanine layer, then whatever remains flows to the junior or equity tranche. Losses travel in the opposite direction. The junior tranche absorbs them first, and senior holders are only touched once the layers below them are gone. This is how one loan book can issue a five percent token and a fourteen percent token at the same time against the same collateral. The gap between those two numbers is the price of your position in the queue, and in a downturn the junior holder is the one who pays it."], "point": "Same collateral does not mean same risk. Find out which tranche you are buying.", "chapter": 5, "note": "junior gets paid more because junior gets hit first", "t": 250.0}, {"id": "5.4", "page": 24, "title": "Fee Structures", "sub": "Fees are paid out of the same yield that was used to sell you the product.", "body": ["Almost every fee in a tokenized product is paid out of the yield you were shown. A management fee is charged on assets under management whether the product performs or not. A performance fee takes a cut of gains above a stated hurdle. Issuance and redemption fees are charged at the door, and they punish short holding periods hardest. Beneath those sit custody, administration, audit and chain costs, sometimes disclosed as one expense figure and sometimes scattered across the offering documents. Advertised yield is usually gross, and net yield is whatever survives that stack after everyone in the chain has been paid."], "point": "Ask for the net number after every fee. If nobody will give you one, that is the answer.", "chapter": 5, "note": "everyone in the chain gets paid. then you.", "t": 259.29}], "tagline": "the number on the billboard vs. the number in your wallet"}, {"n": 6, "id": "credit", "name": "Private Credit", "lessons": [{"id": "6.1", "page": 25, "title": "What Tokenizing Private Credit Means", "sub": "The loan does not change. The wrapper around it does.", "body": ["Private credit is lending that happens away from public bond markets. A fund or a direct lender writes a loan to a business, holds it on its own book, and collects interest until the borrower repays. Tokenizing it means issuing a token that represents a claim on that loan, or on a pool of them.", "The token carries the economics. It does not rewrite them. The borrower still has a repayment schedule, the loan still has a fixed term, and someone still has to underwrite the credit before a single token is minted."], "point": "Tokenizing a loan changes how you hold it. It does not change who owes you.", "chapter": 6, "note": "now with a token. still a loan.", "t": 272.14}, {"id": "6.2", "page": 26, "title": "Who's Building It", "sub": "Not every platform in this sector is doing the same job.", "body": ["Private credit onchain is not one business. Origination platforms underwrite the loans themselves and keep the credit relationship. Figure sits there. Pool platforms like Centrifuge and Maple run the vaults, the tranching and the servicing logic between lender and borrower. Each occupies a different point in the chain, and each answers a different question when a borrower stops paying."], "point": "Find out who underwrote the loan. That is often a different name from whoever sold you the token.", "kind": "builders", "builders": [{"name": "Figure", "logo": "figure"}, {"name": "Centrifuge", "logo": "centrifuge"}, {"name": "Maple", "logo": "maple"}], "chapter": 6, "note2": "A few examples. The full list runs much longer.", "note": "the seller isn't always the underwriter", "t": 280.71}, {"id": "6.3", "page": 27, "title": "Yield Structure & Underwriting", "sub": "A rate is only as good as the credit work sitting behind it.", "body": ["Yield here comes from borrowers paying interest on real loans, not from a protocol handing out rewards. The advertised rate is the coupon the borrower agreed to. What reaches a token holder is that coupon minus servicing costs, management fees, and any loans in the pool that stop performing. Underwriting is the work behind that number. It sets who qualifies to borrow, what collateral backs the loan, and how much cushion sits between the value of that collateral and the debt. When one pool advertises a rate well above the rest of the sector, the gap is usually risk that other lenders looked at and declined."], "point": "Read the fee stack and the collateral before the headline rate. That is where the yield is decided.", "chapter": 6, "note": "they looked. they passed.", "t": 289.29}, {"id": "6.4", "page": 28, "title": "Redemption Terms & Liquidity Reality", "sub": "A redemption window is only as real as the cash sitting behind it.", "body": ["Most private credit tokens advertise redemptions on a schedule, weekly, monthly, or quarterly. That schedule describes intent, not a guarantee. The loans backing the pool do not mature on that same schedule. When redemption requests outpace incoming loan repayments and fresh capital, the manager leans on cash reserves, then on gates that slow or cap withdrawals. Evergreen funds handle this differently than closed term funds, and the difference matters more during stress than in calm markets. Before treating a redemption window as liquidity, check whether it has ever been tested. A rate sheet with a redemption schedule is not the same as a track record of honoring it."], "point": "A redemption window is a policy. Whether it holds under stress is a track record, not a promise.", "chapter": 6, "note": "window: open* *until everyone leaves at once", "t": 297.86}], "tagline": "somebody still has to pay the loan back"}, {"n": 7, "id": "realestate", "name": "Real Estate", "lessons": [{"id": "7.1", "page": 29, "title": "What Tokenized Real Estate Means", "sub": "The building does not move. The ownership record does.", "body": ["Real estate is an asset that is hard to divide and hard to sell fast. A property gets tokenized by placing it inside a legal structure, usually an SPV or trust that holds the deed, then issuing tokens that represent shares in that structure. The token is a claim on the entity that owns the building, not a claim on the building directly.", "The token carries the ownership stake. It does not carry the property itself. Someone still has to hold title, manage the asset, collect rent, and handle the legal work that comes with owning physical property before a single token gets minted."], "point": "Tokenizing a property changes how you hold the stake. It does not change what you own.", "chapter": 7, "note": "can't fit a house in a wallet. we checked.", "t": 310.0}, {"id": "7.2", "page": 30, "title": "Who's Building It", "sub": "Four builders, four different things the token actually holds.", "body": ["Four groups are building tokenized real estate, and they are not building the same product. Lofty fractionalizes single US rental homes on Algorand. Each property sits in its own LLC, tokens represent membership interests in that LLC, rent accrues daily, and minimums are low enough that a buyer can hold a slice of a house for the price of a dinner. Propy works on the transaction layer instead of the ownership layer, putting title and deed records onchain so an entire property changes hands rather than getting sliced into shares. Blocksquare sits underneath other issuers as a protocol layer, linking a token to a notarized claim on the property under EU law and licensing that plumbing to local operators who source the buildings themselves. Balcony goes to the public record itself, partnering with county record offices such as New Jersey's Bergen County to put deeds and the full chain of title onchain, so the land registry, not a private platform, keeps the ledger. Same asset class, four different answers to the question of what the token is actually attached to."], "point": "A membership interest, a deed record, and a protocol claim are three different things. Read which one the token holds.", "kind": "builders", "builders": [{"name": "Lofty", "logo": "lofty"}, {"name": "Propy", "logo": "propy"}, {"name": "Blocksquare", "logo": "blocksquare"}, {"name": "Balcony", "logo": "balcony"}], "chapter": 7, "note": "same asset class. four answers.", "t": 318.57}, {"id": "7.3", "page": 31, "title": "Fractional Ownership Mechanics", "sub": "How a house becomes units, and how units become tokens.", "body": ["You never own the building. You own a piece of a company that owns the building. A sponsor buys the property and places it inside a single purpose entity, an LLC in the United States or an SPV in Europe. That entity holds the deed. Ownership of the entity is divided into a fixed number of units, and each token maps to one unit. Mint one thousand tokens against a $250,000 house and every token carries a $250 claim on what the entity produces. Rent arrives, the operator takes management fees, maintenance reserves, insurance and property tax, and the remainder is distributed to holders on a set cycle. The token is also the share register. Transfer it and ownership updates without a lawyer redrafting the operating agreement, which is where the cost savings actually come from. What the token will not give you is a vote on the roof, a key to the door, or the power to force a sale. Those rights stay with the sponsor, and the operating agreement is where they are written down."], "point": "Read the operating agreement, not the listing. It sets the fees, the payout cycle, and who has the power to sell the building.", "flow": ["PROPERTY", "SPV / LLC", "UNITS", "TOKENS"], "chapter": 7, "note": "your token won't open the front door", "t": 327.14}, {"id": "7.4", "page": 32, "title": "The Liquidity Reality Check", "sub": "Where the exit already works, and where it still needs depth.", "body": ["Tokenization fixes the mechanics of the exit. Selling a stake in a private property deal has always meant lawyers, a broker, a redrafted operating agreement and several months of waiting. A token transfer clears in minutes and updates the share register itself, and the cost of moving a position from one holder to the next falls to almost nothing. What the technology does not hand over is a deep buyer pool. Whitelists limit who can take the other side, holding periods apply on Reg D issues, and most platforms match trades only against their own verified users. The market is thin today and prices can sit under net asset value while it fills out. It is filling out. Regulated venues are opening, platforms are starting to accept each other's investor checks, and lending desks now take property tokens as collateral, which lets a holder raise cash without selling the position at all."], "point": "Measure the exit against a private property sale. On that benchmark, the rails are already ahead.", "flow": ["SELL", "BORROW AGAINST IT", "HOLD TO EXIT"], "chapter": 7, "note": "honestly? kind of great.", "t": 336.43}], "tagline": "you can't put a house in a wallet"}, {"n": 8, "id": "rails", "name": "Infrastructure & Compliance Rails", "lessons": [{"id": "8.1", "page": 33, "title": "Oracles", "sub": "A chain cannot see the world. Something has to report it.", "body": ["A blockchain only knows what has been written to it. It cannot see the price of a Treasury bill, the net asset value of a fund, or whether a property was appraised last month. An oracle is the service that carries offchain information onchain and posts it where a contract can read it.", "In RWA, oracles report prices, net asset values, interest rates, and proof of reserve attestations. A tokenized fund that accrues yield needs its NAV posted before holders see any change. A lending market holding tokenized collateral needs a price to liquidate against. The contract executes without judgment, so whatever the oracle posts becomes the number the system treats as real."], "point": "An oracle does not verify reality. It reports what it was handed, and the contract acts as if that report is fact.", "chapter": 8, "note": "the contract believes everything. zero judgment.", "t": 348.57}, {"id": "8.2", "page": 34, "title": "Bridges", "sub": "A token can cross chains. The claim behind it does not always cross with it.", "body": ["A bridge moves a token from one chain to another. Most do it by locking the token on the first chain and minting a representation on the second, so what arrives is a claim on the locked original rather than the original itself. For RWA that distinction carries weight, because the legal right to the underlying asset sits with the token the issuer recognises on its register. Issuers have started closing the gap by minting natively on several chains from one register, so a holder is recognised wherever they hold. Where that is not offered, the bridge becomes one more counterparty standing between a holder and the asset."], "point": "A bridged token is a claim on a claim. Check which version the issuer will actually redeem.", "chapter": 8, "note": "a claim on a claim on a… you get it", "t": 357.14}, {"id": "8.3", "page": 35, "title": "KYC / AML Onchain", "sub": "Compliance does not disappear when an asset is tokenized. It moves into the token.", "body": ["Know Your Customer and Anti-Money Laundering checks are the identity work a regulated issuer completes before anyone is allowed to hold a security. Onchain, that work does not go away, it gets encoded. A wallet is verified offchain, then added to an allowlist or given a credential the token contract can read. The transfer function checks that record before anything clears, so a transfer to an unverified wallet fails outright rather than surfacing in a review weeks later. The trade is that a permissioned token only moves inside the set of wallets the issuer has already approved."], "point": "Compliance did not go away. It moved into the transfer function, where every trade is checked before it settles.", "chapter": 8, "note": "no awkward email weeks later. it just fails.", "t": 365.71}, {"id": "8.4", "page": 36, "title": "Accredited Investor Gating", "sub": "Verification asks who you are. Accreditation asks whether you are allowed in.", "body": ["Accreditation is a wealth test. In the US it means income above $200,000 or a net worth over $1 million, and it decides which offerings you are permitted to buy. Onchain, that status becomes one more flag on the allowlist that already carries your KYC record, so a single call clears identity and eligibility together. The plumbing is clean. The policy is the problem, because gating by wealth keeps the higher-yielding end of the market closed to almost everyone. Wally and the RWA Foundation back open systems, and walled gardens are what tokenization was built to take apart."], "point": "The rails are already open. The gate is a rulebook, and that is the wall open systems still have to bring down.", "chapter": 8, "note": "everyone else: please enjoy the lobby", "t": 374.29}, {"id": "8.5", "page": 37, "title": "Jurisdiction Arbitrage", "sub": "The token travels anywhere. The rulebook behind it was chosen on purpose.", "body": ["Tokenization is global and securities law is not, so every issuer picks a home. Delaware, Cayman, Liechtenstein, Singapore and Abu Dhabi each come with a different answer on who may buy, what must be disclosed, and which court hears you when the issuer stops paying. Some of that choice is ordinary structuring, since an offshore vehicle is how a product reaches investors US rules would shut out. Some of it is shopping for the thinnest rulebook that still issues a licence. On a marketing page the two are indistinguishable, because the yield quoted is the same number either way."], "point": "Read the domicile before the yield. It decides which court hears your claim and what that claim is worth.", "chapter": 8, "note": "same 8%. very different court.", "t": 382.86}], "tagline": "the plumbing nobody tweets about"}, {"n": 9, "id": "commodities", "name": "Commodities", "lessons": [{"id": "9.1", "page": 38, "title": "What Tokenized Commodities Means", "sub": "The metal stays in the vault. The claim on it moves.", "body": ["A tokenized commodity is a claim on a physical good somebody else holds. Gold is the dominant case. A custodian keeps bars in a vault, an auditor confirms they are there, and each token stands for a stated weight of that metal. Silver and oil work the same way with more friction.", "The token does not change the commodity. It changes who can hold a piece of it and how fast that piece moves. A gram of gold that once needed a broker, a vault account and a settlement window now sits in a wallet. The vault and the redemption desk stay offchain, doing the work."], "point": "A commodity token moves the claim, not the metal. What it is worth depends on the vault behind it.", "chapter": 9, "note": "the gold is not coming with you", "t": 401.43}, {"id": "9.2", "page": 39, "title": "Who's Building It", "sub": "Almost all of it is gold. The vault matters more than the ticker.", "body": ["Tokenized commodities is, in practice, mostly gold, and three issuers carry most of it. Each token is a claim on bars held with a custodian. The vault operators and refiners do the part that decides whether any of it holds, storing allocated metal to LBMA standards and letting auditors count it. Redemption is where these issuers separate."], "point": "A metal token is only as good as the vault behind it. Ask who audits it and who can take delivery.", "kind": "builders", "builders": [{"name": "PAX Gold", "logo": "paxg"}, {"name": "Tether Gold", "logo": "xaut"}, {"name": "Matrixdock", "logo": "matrixdock"}], "chapter": 9, "note2": "Silver, platinum and oil exist onchain too, at a fraction of the size.", "note": "the vault matters more than the ticker", "t": 410.0}, {"id": "9.3", "page": 40, "title": "Custody & Redemption", "sub": "The token moves in seconds. The bar moves on someone else's terms.", "body": ["Allocation is the first question. Allocated metal means specific bars, listed by serial number, assigned to the token supply and held in an LBMA vault by a custodian like Brink's or Malca-Amit. Security carries as much weight as the paperwork, armoured vaults, segregated compartments and insurance at full replacement value. Unallocated means a claim on a pool, ranking with every other creditor if the issuer fails. Redemption then splits by size. Cash settlement is open to most holders, physical delivery needs a full bar, full KYC, and shipping at your cost."], "point": "Custody is who holds the bar. Redemption is whether you can ever get it.", "chapter": 9, "note2": "An independent physical count is what makes any of this checkable.", "note": "bring a wheelbarrow", "t": 418.57}], "tagline": "it's mostly gold. ask about the vault."}, {"n": 10, "id": "funds", "name": "Funds", "lessons": [{"id": "10.1", "page": 41, "title": "What Tokenizing Funds Means", "sub": "The fund keeps its rules. The share gets new rails.", "body": ["A tokenized fund is an ordinary fund whose shares are issued as tokens. The manager still runs the portfolio, the administrator still strikes the NAV, and the transfer agent still keeps the register. The token is that register entry, written onchain. Money market funds lead the way.", "Tokenizing a fund leaves the strategy alone and rebuilds the plumbing around it. Subscriptions and redemptions that took days to pass through intermediaries can settle in minutes, and a share can sit in a wallet or back a loan. The legal wrapper and the regulator stay where they were."], "point": "A fund token is the same share on faster rails. What it is worth depends on the fund behind it.", "chapter": 10, "note": "money market funds lead the way", "t": 430.71}, {"id": "10.2", "page": 42, "title": "Who's Building It", "sub": "A simple guide to who does what in a tokenized fund.", "body": ["The companies building tokenized funds fall into three groups. Big asset managers take funds they already run and sell shares as tokens. Crypto-native firms launch new funds that exist onchain from the start. Tokenization platforms work behind both, creating the tokens and keeping the record of who owns each share."], "point": "Before you buy, check who keeps the record of who owns the shares.", "kind": "builders", "groups": [{"label": "ASSET MANAGERS", "items": [{"name": "BlackRock", "logo": "blackrock"}, {"name": "Franklin Templeton", "logo": "franklin"}, {"name": "Apollo", "logo": "apollo"}]}, {"label": "CRYPTO-NATIVE", "items": [{"name": "Ondo", "logo": "ondo"}, {"name": "Superstate", "logo": "superstate"}, {"name": "Spiko", "logo": "spiko"}]}, {"label": "PLATFORMS", "items": [{"name": "Securitize", "logo": "securitize"}, {"name": "Centrifuge", "logo": "centrifuge2"}, {"name": "Libeara", "logo": "libeara"}]}], "chapter": 10, "note": "a simple guide to who does what", "t": 439.29}, {"id": "10.3", "page": 43, "title": "Yield & Redemption", "sub": "How a tokenized fund pays you, and how you get out.", "body": ["The yield on a tokenized fund comes from the assets the fund holds, not from the token. Redemption is the other half of the deal. It is the process of handing your shares back to the issuer and getting cash returned. One runs on the market's clock. The other runs on the fund's."], "point": "Selling a token is instant. Redeeming it for cash runs on the fund's calendar.", "sections": [{"h": "WHERE THE YIELD COMES FROM", "t": "Treasury bills pay interest. Loans pay coupons. Property pays rent. The token is a claim on that income, never a source of it."}, {"h": "HOW THE YIELD REACHES YOU", "t": "Some funds mint extra tokens into your wallet. Others hold the income inside the fund so each token climbs in price. Same money, different plumbing."}, {"h": "HOW YOU GET YOUR CASH BACK", "t": "You file a redemption request. The issuer burns your tokens, sells assets or uses cash on hand, and wires the proceeds. Daily for some funds, quarterly for others."}], "chapter": 10, "note": "sell: now. redeem: when the fund says so.", "t": 447.86}, {"id": "10.4", "page": 44, "title": "Open vs Closed-End", "sub": "Two fund shapes. Two very different ways out.", "body": ["An open-end fund makes new shares whenever money arrives and cancels them when money leaves. A closed-end fund raises once, shuts the door, and runs to a maturity date. The assets underneath decide which shape a fund takes."], "point": "Open-end funds redeem. Closed-end funds mature. A token does not change which one you hold.", "sections": [{"h": "HOW AN OPEN-END FUND WORKS", "t": "Shares are created and redeemed on demand at net asset value. Treasury and money market funds work like this because their assets sell in a day."}, {"h": "HOW A CLOSED-END FUND WORKS", "t": "Capital is raised once, then locked until the fund winds down. Private credit and property need that, since the assets take years to exit."}, {"h": "WHAT THE TOKEN CHANGES", "t": "A token can make a closed-end fund tradable without making it redeemable. You sell to another buyer, never back to the issuer."}], "chapter": 10, "note": "tradable ≠ redeemable", "t": 456.43}], "tagline": "same fund. faster pipes."}, {"n": 11, "id": "risk", "name": "Risk & Red Flags", "lessons": [{"id": "11.1", "page": 45, "title": "Why Self-Reported Metrics Get Flagged", "sub": "A number is only as solid as whoever checked it.", "body": ["A self-reported metric is any figure a project publishes about itself: assets, holders, yield, volume. Publishing them is normal. The trouble is that nobody outside the project has checked them, and the project has every reason to present them well.", "RWA widens the gap. Token supply is readable onchain, but the asset behind it sits offchain with a custodian. A headline figure could describe a live fund, a signed mandate or a pipeline slide. The flag tells you whether you are reading verified onchain data or the issuer's word."], "point": "A flag does not call the number false. It says nobody outside the issuer has checked it.", "chapter": 11, "note": "source: trust me bro", "t": 475.0}, {"id": "11.2", "page": 46, "title": "What Verified Means", "sub": "Audits, attestations and proof of reserve check different things.", "body": ["Verified can mean three different checks in RWA, and each one leaves something out. A round $500M figure with no report behind it is none of them. Before you trust the word, ask which check was run, and when."], "point": "An attestation is a snapshot of one day. Always ask who checked the number, and when.", "sections": [{"h": "AUDIT", "t": "An independent firm tests the books, assets and controls across a full year, then signs an opinion. Thorough, but months old by the time you read it."}, {"h": "ATTESTATION", "t": "An accountant confirms one claim on one date, such as reserves covering supply at month end. It says nothing about the day after."}, {"h": "PROOF OF RESERVE", "t": "An oracle checks the custodian's balance against token supply onchain, often live. If the custodian's number is wrong, the feed repeats it."}], "chapter": 11, "note": "audit = a physical. attestation = a selfie.", "t": 483.57}, {"id": "11.3", "page": 47, "title": "Who Holds the Keys", "sub": "Admin keys decide who can mint, freeze or upgrade your token.", "body": ["For many backed tokenized assets, securing the underlying asset is only half the story. The other half is the smart contract, and someone controls its admin permissions. Those permissions can change what happens to your token after you buy it."], "point": "A multisig and a timelock are the brakes. Ask who holds the keys, and what stops them.", "sections": [{"h": "MINT", "t": "Creates new tokens. For a fully backed asset, each mint should match new backing. If one wallet can mint unchecked, supply can outrun the assets."}, {"h": "FREEZE", "t": "Pauses transfers or blocks a single address, usually for sanctions or court orders. That is compliance working, and it can reach your wallet too."}, {"h": "UPGRADE", "t": "For an upgradeable contract, this can swap the code behind the token while its address stays the same. Fixes ship fast, and so can new rules."}], "chapter": 11, "note": "admin key: one guy named dave", "t": 492.86}, {"id": "11.4", "page": 48, "title": "The Trust Chain", "sub": "Every link between you and the asset is someone you rely on.", "body": ["A token is a claim, and a claim is only as good as whoever has to honour it. Onchain removes some middlemen, not all of them. The ones left are counterparties, and if one fails, your token can stay onchain and still lose value."], "point": "Every link is a counterparty. Name each one, then ask what happens if it breaks.", "sections": [{"h": "ISSUER", "t": "Owes you redemption. Check the assets sit in a bankruptcy-remote SPV, so the issuer's creditors cannot reach them."}, {"h": "CUSTODIAN", "t": "Holds the backing. If it can lend or re-pledge those assets, known as rehypothecation, someone else has a claim on them too."}, {"h": "RAILS", "t": "Oracles price the token, bridges move it, a transfer agent keeps the register. Any one can be wrong, offline or hacked."}], "chapter": 11, "note": "onchain ≠ okay", "t": 501.43}], "tagline": "the part they skip in the pitch deck"}], "herd": {"page": 49, "eyebrow": "THE HERD", "title": "RWA Bulls", "sub": "Forty-eight pages of reading done. Now go follow these twelve.", "point": "The book stops at page 49. The herd keeps moving.", "accounts": [{"name": "RWA Foundation", "handle": "RWAFoundation_"}, {"name": "Happy", "handle": "happytokenizing"}, {"name": "Zeus", "handle": "ZeusRWA"}, {"name": "Ray Buckton", "handle": "RayBuckton"}, {"name": "Scofield", "handle": "ScofieldOnchain"}, {"name": "Diego", "handle": "diegoxyz"}, {"name": "stein", "handle": "steinRWA"}, {"name": "Harry Tran", "handle": "HarryTran_RWA"}, {"name": "Zinn", "handle": "zinnresearch"}, {"name": "RWA Llama", "handle": "RwaLlama"}, {"name": "incept1oN", "handle": "_incept1on"}, {"name": "Pavel Yurich", "handle": "PavelYuRichRWA"}]}, "pages": 49};
